<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arctic Ocean</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Arctic Ocean is the smallest and shallowest of the world's oceans, surrounding the North Pole and bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. Much of it is covered by sea ice for most of the year. Between 1920 and 1944 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> reached it through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petsamo">Petsamo</a> corridor, and its northern sea routes have grown in strategic and economic importance as the ice has retreated.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arctic-ocean.jpg" alt="Arctic Ocean" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CIA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arctic_Ocean_-_en.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Arctic Ocean is the smallest and shallowest of the world's five oceanic divisions, with an area of approximately 14,060,000 km2, and the coldest of them. The International Hydrographic Organization recognizes it as an ocean, although some oceanographers call it the Arctic Mediterranean Sea or North Polar Sea. It includes the North Pole region and extends south to about 60°N, surrounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>. Its borders follow topographic features: the Bering Strait on the Pacific side and the Greenland Scotland Ridge on the Atlantic side.</p><p>The ocean occupies a roughly circular basin, almost the size of Antarctica, with a coastline 45,390 km long. It is partially covered by sea ice throughout the year and almost completely in winter. Its salinity is the lowest on average of the five oceans, owing to low evaporation, heavy fresh water inflow from rivers and limited connection to saltier waters. It joins the Pacific through the Bering Strait and the Atlantic through the Greenland Sea and Labrador Sea. Its largest seas include the Barents Sea at 1.4 million km2, the Greenland Sea, the East Siberian Sea and the Kara Sea.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Human habitation in the North American polar region goes back at least 17,000 to 50,000 years, to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wisconsin">Wisconsin</a> glaciation, when falling sea levels let people cross the Bering land bridge from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a>. Early Paleo-Eskimo groups included the Pre-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a> (c. 3200-850 BCE), the Saqqaq culture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a> (2500-800 BCE) and the Independence I and II cultures. The Dorset culture spread across Arctic North America between 500 BCE and 1500 CE. The Thule people, who arose around the Bering Strait from about 200 BCE and spread across almost the whole North American Arctic, were the ancestors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inuit">Inuit</a>.</p><p>For much of European history the north polar regions remained largely unexplored and their geography conjectural. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pytheas">Pytheas</a> of Massilia recorded a journey northward in 325 BCE to a land he called Eschate Thule, where the Sun set for only three hours a day and the water was replaced by a congealed substance. He was probably describing loose sea ice. Early cartographers were unsure how to draw the pole: Johannes Ruysch's map of 1507 and Gerardus Mercator's of 1595 included land, while Martin Waldseemüller's world map of 1507 showed only water. Expeditions added small islands such as Novaya Zemlya and Spitzbergen, and from the late 16th to the early 20th century they became more common, often in search of trade routes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to the Pacific.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arctic Ocean” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
